---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import ChainTable from "./_lib/ChainTable.astro";
import ChainLimits from "./_lib/ChainLimits.astro";
import {
  CHAIN_PAGES, itemsFor, studyRank, chainSchemas, usd, round,
  PARENT_PATH, CSV_PATH, STUDY_SIZE, STUDY_CHAINS, ALL_RANKED,
} from "./_lib/fastfood";

const page = CHAIN_PAGES.wendys;
const items = itemsFor(page.chain);

const best = items[0];
const worst = items[items.length - 1];
const spread = best.perDollar / worst.perDollar;

const jrBacon = items.find((i) => i.item === "Jr. Bacon Cheeseburger")!;
const spicyChicken = items.find((i) => i.item === "Spicy Chicken Sandwich")!;
const davesSingle = items.find((i) => i.item === "Dave's Single")!;
const chili = items.find((i) => i.item.startsWith("Chili"))!;

const jrBaconStudyRank = studyRank(jrBacon);

/**
 * Is Wendy's really the tightest menu in the study, or does it just feel that
 * way? Computed rather than asserted: best/worst ratio within every chain that
 * has at least three priced rows.
 */
const chainSpreads = [...new Set(ALL_RANKED.map((i) => i.chain))]
  .map((chain) => {
    const rows = ALL_RANKED.filter((i) => i.chain === chain);
    return { chain, n: rows.length, spread: rows[0].perDollar / rows[rows.length - 1].perDollar };
  })
  .filter((c) => c.n >= 3)
  .sort((a, b) => a.spread - b.spread);
const tightest = chainSpreads[0];
const widest = chainSpreads[chainSpreads.length - 1];
const isTightest = tightest.chain === page.chain;

const title = `Wendy's Protein per Dollar: ${items.length} Items Ranked (July 2026 Prices)`;
const description =
  `Every Wendy's item we priced, ranked by grams of protein per dollar, from the ${best.item} at ` +
  `${best.perDollar} g per $1 down to ${worst.perDollar}. The tightest menu in our fast food study. ` +
  `Prices from Wendy's own national menu, observed ${page.observed}. No calorie figures, because our dataset has none.`;

const schemas = chainSchemas({ page, items, title, description });
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={schemas}>
  <Header />
  <main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 md:py-16">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="text-sm mb-6" style="color: var(--muted-color);">
      <a href="/" class="underline" style="color:#F29B30;">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
      <a href={PARENT_PATH} class="underline" style="color:#F29B30;">Fast food protein per dollar</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
      <span>Wendy's</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-4" style="color: var(--heading-color);">
      Wendy's Protein per Dollar, Ranked
    </h1>

    <p class="text-lg mb-4 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
      Wendy's is the one chain in this study where the order barely matters. Every item we priced lands between
      {" "}{worst.perDollar} and {best.perDollar} grams of protein per dollar, a spread of just
      {" "}{round(spread, 2)}x{isTightest ? `, the tightest of any chain we priced` : ""}. For comparison,
      {" "}{widest.chain} runs a {round(widest.spread, 1)}-fold gap between its best and worst rows. Here are all
      {" "}{items.length} Wendy's items we priced, with the date and source on every number.
    </p>

    <p class="text-sm mb-8 p-4 rounded-xl" style="color: var(--muted-color); background: var(--card-bg); border:1px solid var(--border-color);">
      <strong style="color:var(--heading-color);">Prices observed {page.observed}</strong>, and these are the
      best-sourced prices in the whole study: they come from Wendy's own national baseline menu on
      order.wendys.com on a single stated day, a la carte, rather than from a third-party tracker averaging
      other people's guesses. Protein grams come from the same national menu nutrition data. Franchise pricing
      still varies by location. Full data in the
      <a href={CSV_PATH} class="underline" style="color:#F29B30;">raw CSV</a>.
    </p>

    <dl class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="ff-stat">
        <dt>Best value</dt>
        <dd>{best.perDollar} g / $1</dd>
        <p>{best.item}, {best.proteinG} g for {usd(best.priceUsd)}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ff-stat">
        <dt>Worst value</dt>
        <dd>{worst.perDollar} g / $1</dd>
        <p>{worst.item}, {worst.proteinG} g for {usd(worst.priceUsd)}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ff-stat">
        <dt>Spread on one menu</dt>
        <dd>{round(spread, 2)}x</dd>
        <p>tightest of the {chainSpreads.length} chains with enough rows to compare</p>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <section id="ranking">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-3" style="color: var(--heading-color);">
        All {items.length} Wendy's items, best protein value first
      </h2>
      <p class="mb-6" style="color: var(--text-color);">
        One division problem per row: protein grams divided by the price. The rank column shows where each item
        lands in the full {STUDY_SIZE}-item study across all {STUDY_CHAINS} chains.
      </p>
      <ChainTable items={items} chain={page.chain} observed={page.observed} studyRankOf={studyRank} />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-4" style="color: var(--heading-color);">
        What this actually means when you're standing at the counter
      </h2>

      <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
        Order whatever you want. That's a genuinely unusual conclusion for one of these pages, and it's what the
        numbers say. At McDonald's the gap between the smart order and the dumb one is nearly two to one. At
        Chipotle it's three to one. At Wendy's it's {round(spread, 2)} to one, which is close enough to nothing
        that picking by ratio would be a strange way to run your life.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
        If you want the technically correct answer, it's the {jrBacon.item}: {jrBacon.proteinG} grams for
        {" "}{usd(jrBacon.priceUsd)}, which is {jrBacon.perDollar} grams per dollar and the number
        {" "}{jrBaconStudyRank} row in the entire {STUDY_SIZE}-item study. It's also the cheapest thing on this
        page. That keeps happening across chains, and it's the most useful pattern in the whole dataset: the
        small cheap burger nobody photographs quietly beats the flagship almost everywhere.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
        The {chili.item} deserves a mention because it's doing something none of the others are.
        {" "}{chili.proteinG} grams for {usd(chili.priceUsd)} at {chili.perDollar} grams per dollar, which puts
        it level with the {davesSingle.item} while costing two dollars less. It's beans and beef in a cup, so
        the ratio is not a shock. If you're at Wendy's and you want protein without a bun involved, that's the
        order, and it's the closest thing to a grocery-store food anybody on this menu sells.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
        The {davesSingle.item} and the {spicyChicken.item} land at {davesSingle.perDollar} and
        {" "}{spicyChicken.perDollar}, which is to say they land in the same place. The chicken sandwich has
        {" "}{spicyChicken.proteinG} grams, the burger has {davesSingle.proteinG}, and the price difference
        cancels the protein difference almost exactly. Pick on taste.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
        One caution about the headline finding, because it's the kind of thing that's easy to oversell. We
        priced {items.length} Wendy's items, not the Wendy's menu. A tight spread across four items is real but
        it's four items, and there are plenty of things behind that counter, from breakfast to the big combo
        stacks, that we didn't price and that could easily sit outside this band. What the four do show is that
        Wendy's core lineup is consistently decent value, which is more than most of the chains here manage.
      </p>

      <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
        And the usual scale check: {best.perDollar} grams per dollar is a good drive-thru number and a poor
        number generally. The
        <a href="/cheapest-protein-per-gram/" class="underline font-semibold" style="color:#F29B30;">cheapest protein per gram ranking</a>
        {" "}prices the raw ingredients, and the top of that list makes this whole page look like a rounding error.
      </p>
    </section>

    <ChainLimits chain={page.chain} itemCount={items.length} observed={page.observed} />

    <section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-4" style="color: var(--heading-color);">
        Compare it to another menu
      </h2>
      <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
        A consistent menu is only interesting next to an inconsistent one.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        {page.siblings.map((s) => (
          <li class="leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
            <a href={s.path} class="underline font-semibold" style="color:#F29B30;">{s.label} protein per dollar</a>: {s.why}.
          </li>
        ))}
        <li class="leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
          <a href={PARENT_PATH} class="underline font-semibold" style="color:#F29B30;">The full {STUDY_SIZE}-item fast food ranking</a>: all {STUDY_CHAINS} chains in one table, plus how the best drive-thru order compares to a bag of dry beans.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />

  <style>
    .ff-stat { padding:1rem 1.1rem; border-radius:.9rem; background:var(--card-bg); border:1px solid var(--border-color); }
    .ff-stat dt { font-size:.72rem; font-weight:700; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.06em; color:var(--muted-color); margin-bottom:.25rem; }
    .ff-stat dd { font-size:1.9rem; font-weight:800; color:#F29B30; line-height:1.1; }
    .ff-stat p { font-size:.82rem; color:var(--muted-color); margin-top:.3rem; }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
